  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 129 PM CDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nAn abnormally strong upper-level ridge is centered over the Four\nCorners region today, and looks to remain there through early to\nmid week at least. To the east, the Bermuda High remains in place\nover the western Atlantic. But that will actually retrograde \ntoward the southeastern CONUS and Gulf Coast by early next week. \nMeanwhile, westerly flow with the jet stream will remain deflected\nacross the northern CONUS until later next week when a more \npronounced ridge-trough pattern establishes over the country.\n\nOur forecast area and broader region will therefore remain\n\"mostly\" beneath the weak troughing pattern between the ridges \ninto next week. PWAT values remain soupy with onshore flow and \ndeep tropical moisture being reinforced with an easterly wave \nSaturday into Sunday. Given the lack of strong ridging overhead, \nreinforcing moisture influx, and daily chances for showers and \nstorms, the confidence in widespread 105-110 heat index values is \nnot great enough to justify Heat Advisory headlines at this point \nin time. Peak heat index values each day will be near 105 degrees,\nespecially over the next several days. \"Limited\" heat stress \nmessaging is in line with the expected forecast, but this kind of \nhot and humid weather is still dangerous for sensitive \npopulations, especially the elderly or people without functioning \nair conditioning in the home. Care should be taken to limit \nstrenuous activities in mid day, and frequent breaks with cool \nwater and shade are advised for anyone working or playing outdoors\nthis time of year. By later next week, we may see some\nstrengthening of the ridge overhead, and this could result in\ngreater heat stress levels. /NF/EC/",
